a%22%20stroke-width%3D%222%22%20marker-end%3D%22url%28%23a%29%22%2F%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22218%22%20y%3D%22210%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%2290%22%20rx%3D%2210%22%20fill%3D%22%23eff6ff%22%20stroke%3D%22%231e3a8a%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%22234%22%20cy%3D%22226%22%20r%3D%2211%22%20fill%3D%22%231e3a8a%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22234%22%20y%3D%22230%22%20font-size%3D%2212%22%20fill%3D%22%23ffffff%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3E7%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22293.0%22%20y%3D%22246%22%20font-size%3D%2212%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EAdult%20frog%20%2812-16%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22293.0%22%20y%3D%22261%22%20font-size%3D%2212%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3Eweeks%29%3C%2Ftext%3E%3C%2Fg%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E', 'The life cycle of a frog
The life cycle of a frog.
Stage 1: Adult frog lays eggs in water
Stage 2: Eggs hatch after about 10 days
Stage 3: Tadpole with gills lives in water
Stage 4: Back legs grow (5-6 weeks)
Stage 5: Front legs appear and tail shortens (9 weeks)
Stage 6: Young frog leaves the water
Stage 7: Adult frog (12-16 weeks)
Main features: The frog’s life cycle has distinct stages over roughly three to four months, starting and ending with adult frogs in a continuous cycle. It begins in the water as eggs and gill-breathing tadpoles, which gradually grow legs and lose their tails before leaving the water as young frogs.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
